// In Rust `take(s)` makes s, and so the array's elements and the Vec's,
// i64: the sums are 4000000000. vole types the literals i32 (the default)
// because s: i32 is accepted by take(x: i64) through the implicit
// widening, so the sum is computed in i32 and widened after: -O0 panics
// "attempt to add with overflow", -O2 prints -294967296 twice. The guide's
// promise (guide rust: a Rust program that is not vole is an error that
// names the fix, "not another meaning") does not hold here. 0.27.8 printed
// 4000000000.
// (0.30.1) The fix is the guide's promise, an error that names the
// annotation: an i32 computed from literals nothing gives a type is not
// widened (type.widen_i32, front-rust.md F29.3). rustc prints 4000000000
// twice; typing the literals from the use is 0.31's.
// (0.30.2) The literals are typed from the use (F18.5): rustc's output.
// EXPECT: 4000000000
// EXPECT: 4000000000
#[inline(never)]
fn take(x: i64) -> i64 {
    x
}

fn main(gpa: Gpa) {
    let arr = [2000000000, 2];
    let s = arr[0] + arr[0];
    println("{}", take(s));
    let mut v = Vec::new_in(gpa);
    v.push(2000000000);
    v.push(arr[1]);
    let t = v[0] + v[0];
    println("{}", take(t));
}
